1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,953
What was the Berlin Tunnel?

2
00:00:02,423 --> 00:00:04,561
It was a secret passage the CIA and

3
00:00:04,581 --> 00:00:07,089
British intelligence dug under the border

4
00:00:07,109 --> 00:00:09,679
into Soviet East Berlin, to tap the Soviet

5
00:00:09,699 --> 00:00:11,344
military's telephone lines.

6
00:00:11,914 --> 00:00:14,819
It was 1,476 feet long.

7
00:00:15,289 --> 00:00:17,735
For 11 months and 11 days, it listened.

8
00:00:18,305 --> 00:00:21,256
Then, at about two in the morning on April

9
00:00:21,276 --> 00:00:23,944
22, 1956, Soviet and East German crews

10
00:00:23,964 --> 00:00:26,773
digging up a faulty cable broke into the

11
00:00:26,793 --> 00:00:27,621
tap chamber.

12
00:00:28,091 --> 00:00:29,953
A hidden microphone picked up their

13
00:00:29,973 --> 00:00:30,329
voices.

14
00:00:30,899 --> 00:00:33,066
For more than 13 hours it recorded them

15
00:00:33,086 --> 00:00:34,860
working out what they had found.

16
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:36,534
The cable is tapped.

17
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:39,135
Unless one had seen it for oneself, nobody

18
00:00:39,155 --> 00:00:40,005
would believe it.

19
00:00:40,575 --> 00:00:42,856
But the CIA later learned something worse.

20
00:00:43,439 --> 00:00:45,493
The Soviets had known about the tunnel

21
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:47,512
months before the digging even began.

22
00:00:48,082 --> 00:00:50,016
This is the file on the Berlin Tunnel.

23
00:00:50,486 --> 00:00:53,171
The CIA's own secret history of the

24
00:00:53,191 --> 00:00:55,258
operation, written in 1968.

25
00:00:55,828 --> 00:00:57,101
Let's open the file.

26
00:01:03,428 --> 00:01:04,621
It started with a gap.

27
00:01:05,241 --> 00:01:08,122
In the late 1940s, the CIA's history says,

28
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:10,747
American intelligence lost some of its

29
00:01:10,767 --> 00:01:13,511
sources, just as the Cold War escalated.

30
00:01:13,981 --> 00:01:16,202
Tapping Soviet phone lines might fill the

31
00:01:16,222 --> 00:01:16,476
gaps.

32
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:18,654
Berlin was the place to try.

33
00:01:19,124 --> 00:01:21,448
The city sat deep inside the Soviet zone

34
00:01:21,468 --> 00:01:22,093
of Germany.

35
00:01:22,563 --> 00:01:25,029
And according to the CIA, East Berlin was

36
00:01:25,049 --> 00:01:26,908
a hub of Soviet communications.

37
00:01:27,478 --> 00:01:30,160
In 1951, an agent network got inside the

38
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,660
East Berlin office of the East German

39
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:33,876
telephone service.

40
00:01:34,346 --> 00:01:36,355
It delivered the details of the Soviet

41
00:01:36,375 --> 00:01:36,835
circuits.

42
00:01:37,305 --> 00:01:39,144
Subsequent events, the history says,

43
00:01:39,164 --> 00:01:40,744
proved them completely correct.

44
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:43,738
A test tap recorded samples for about six

45
00:01:43,758 --> 00:01:44,155
months.

46
00:01:44,625 --> 00:01:46,784
Somewhat ironically, the first actual

47
00:01:46,804 --> 00:01:49,081
material recovered was a recording of a

48
00:01:49,101 --> 00:01:51,496
student teletypist practicing on the home

49
00:01:51,516 --> 00:01:51,791
keys.

50
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:54,055
The British had done this before.

51
00:01:54,525 --> 00:01:57,104
In 1951, they told the CIA they had been

52
00:01:57,124 --> 00:01:59,703
tapping Soviet cables through tunnels in

53
00:01:59,723 --> 00:02:01,132
Vienna for some years.

54
00:02:01,602 --> 00:02:03,627
Berlin, they suggested, might offer the

55
00:02:03,647 --> 00:02:04,256
same chance.

56
00:02:05,176 --> 00:02:06,641
The answer was a warehouse.

57
00:02:07,261 --> 00:02:09,566
The site was in Rudow, on the southeastern

58
00:02:09,586 --> 00:02:11,115
edge of the American sector.

59
00:02:11,585 --> 00:02:13,969
Just across the border, under a main road

60
00:02:13,989 --> 00:02:16,198
to Schoenefeld airport, ran the target

61
00:02:16,218 --> 00:02:16,608
cables.

62
00:02:17,178 --> 00:02:18,531
Three questions remained.

63
00:02:19,001 --> 00:02:21,525
Could a tunnel of about 1,500 feet be dug

64
00:02:21,545 --> 00:02:23,759
in secret, under a heavily patrolled

65
00:02:23,779 --> 00:02:24,194
border?

66
00:02:24,664 --> 00:02:26,796
Where would 3,000 tons of sand go?

67
00:02:27,266 --> 00:02:29,246
And what could stand there without raising

68
00:02:29,266 --> 00:02:29,723
suspicion?

69
00:02:30,293 --> 00:02:32,225
The sand, the history says, haunted the

70
00:02:32,245 --> 00:02:33,427
planners for many weeks.

71
00:02:33,897 --> 00:02:36,090
Until someone suggested, facetiously, that

72
00:02:36,110 --> 00:02:38,146
they dig a hole and put the dirt in it.

73
00:02:38,716 --> 00:02:40,757
Local engineers were told it had been

74
00:02:40,777 --> 00:02:42,930
decided to try a new type of warehouse,

75
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:44,992
half above the ground and half below.

76
00:02:45,462 --> 00:02:47,536
So the basement was dug under the eyes of

77
00:02:47,556 --> 00:02:49,630
the border guards, and we had our hole to

78
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:50,447
put the dirt in.

79
00:02:51,017 --> 00:02:53,683
The CIA's later account says the compound

80
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:56,239
was presented as a U.S. Air Force radar

81
00:02:56,259 --> 00:02:56,567
site.

82
00:02:57,137 --> 00:02:58,906
The other side watched it closely.

83
00:02:59,376 --> 00:03:01,719
The sight of the Soviets and East Germans

84
00:03:01,739 --> 00:03:04,081
standing on top of the tunnel, binoculars

85
00:03:04,101 --> 00:03:06,271
focused on the roof, the history says,

86
00:03:06,291 --> 00:03:08,461
provided considerable amusement to the

87
00:03:08,481 --> 00:03:09,268
people inside.

88
00:03:10,188 --> 00:03:11,370
Then they had to dig.

89
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:14,388
In December 1953, CIA Director Allen

90
00:03:14,408 --> 00:03:17,076
Dulles approved the terms for talks with

91
00:03:17,096 --> 00:03:17,882
the British.

92
00:03:18,352 --> 00:03:20,450
The Americans would build the site, drive

93
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:22,206
the tunnel and record the signals.

94
00:03:22,676 --> 00:03:24,226
The British would make the taps.

95
00:03:24,796 --> 00:03:26,752
For the digging, Dulles went to the Army.

96
00:03:27,222 --> 00:03:29,715
It chose Lieutenant Colonel Leslie Gross,

97
00:03:29,735 --> 00:03:32,289
who built a practice tunnel some 150 yards

98
00:03:32,309 --> 00:03:34,434
long at a high-security base in New

99
00:03:34,454 --> 00:03:34,863
Mexico.

100
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:37,944
One hundred 25 tons of steel tunnel liner

101
00:03:37,964 --> 00:03:40,290
crossed East Germany by freight train.

102
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:42,738
The loss of any package, the history says,

103
00:03:42,758 --> 00:03:44,118
could have blown the project.

104
00:03:44,688 --> 00:03:47,249
In late August 1954, a shaft was started

105
00:03:47,269 --> 00:03:48,926
in the warehouse basement.

106
00:03:49,396 --> 00:03:51,973
Ground water appeared at 16 feet, instead

107
00:03:51,993 --> 00:03:53,240
of the predicted 32.

108
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:56,094
The diggers worked behind a steel shield,

109
00:03:56,114 --> 00:03:58,286
with horizontal blinds to stop the sand

110
00:03:58,306 --> 00:03:58,848
caving in.

111
00:03:59,318 --> 00:04:01,546
Hydraulic jacks pushed it forward, and a

112
00:04:01,566 --> 00:04:03,625
new ring of steel liner was bolted in

113
00:04:03,645 --> 00:04:03,962
place.

114
00:04:04,532 --> 00:04:06,695
Each time East German guards walked their

115
00:04:06,715 --> 00:04:08,558
patrol over the tunnel, the digging

116
00:04:08,578 --> 00:04:08,984
stopped.

117
00:04:09,454 --> 00:04:11,482
The sand rode back to the basement on

118
00:04:11,502 --> 00:04:13,585
trailers behind a converted fork lift.

119
00:04:14,155 --> 00:04:16,314
To fix their position, the engineers

120
00:04:16,334 --> 00:04:18,857
organized a baseball game, to knock a ball

121
00:04:18,877 --> 00:04:21,156
as far into the East Zone as possible.

122
00:04:21,738 --> 00:04:23,851
It failed, the history says, because of

123
00:04:23,870 --> 00:04:25,764
the friendliness of the East German

124
00:04:25,784 --> 00:04:27,951
guards, who kept returning the baseball.

125
00:04:28,521 --> 00:04:31,944
The tunnel was completed on February 28,

126
00:04:31,964 --> 00:04:32,375
1955.

127
00:04:32,845 --> 00:04:35,012
One thousand four hundred seventy-six

128
00:04:35,032 --> 00:04:35,308
feet.

129
00:04:35,778 --> 00:04:37,333
More than a quarter of a mile.

130
00:04:37,903 --> 00:04:40,104
No one, the history says, had ever

131
00:04:40,124 --> 00:04:42,782
tunneled that far in secret, expecting to

132
00:04:42,802 --> 00:04:45,329
hit a target two inches in diameter, 27

133
00:04:45,349 --> 00:04:48,072
inches below a main German Soviet highway.

134
00:04:48,992 --> 00:04:50,030
Now came the tap.

135
00:04:50,650 --> 00:04:52,876
British engineers dug the final shaft up

136
00:04:52,896 --> 00:04:53,774
toward the road.

137
00:04:54,244 --> 00:04:57,261
By March 28, 1955, the three target cables

138
00:04:57,281 --> 00:05:00,226
lay exposed in a chamber just beneath it.

139
00:05:00,796 --> 00:05:03,003
The chamber was insulated so it would not

140
00:05:03,023 --> 00:05:03,872
act like a drum.

141
00:05:04,342 --> 00:05:06,771
Even so, the history says, it was a weird

142
00:05:06,791 --> 00:05:09,161
sensation to be inside when an iron-shod

143
00:05:09,181 --> 00:05:10,415
horse trotted across.

144
00:05:10,985 --> 00:05:13,238
One foggy morning, the chamber microphone

145
00:05:13,258 --> 00:05:14,402
picked up dull thuds.

146
00:05:14,872 --> 00:05:16,943
When the fog burned away, East German

147
00:05:16,963 --> 00:05:19,146
police had set up a checkpoint right on

148
00:05:19,166 --> 00:05:19,372
top.

149
00:05:19,842 --> 00:05:21,966
The officer in charge was stomping his

150
00:05:21,986 --> 00:05:22,981
feet to keep warm.

151
00:05:23,551 --> 00:05:25,336
Everyone knew it would end one day.

152
00:05:25,806 --> 00:05:27,748
The tunnel was mined with demolition

153
00:05:27,768 --> 00:05:28,946
charges at the border.

154
00:05:29,416 --> 00:05:31,546
And if it was found, the American posture

155
00:05:31,566 --> 00:05:33,643
would be flat denial of any knowledge of

156
00:05:33,663 --> 00:05:34,219
the tunnel.

157
00:05:34,790 --> 00:05:37,058
A heavy steel and concrete door sealed the

158
00:05:37,078 --> 00:05:38,148
tunnel near its end.

159
00:05:38,731 --> 00:05:40,872
It carried a sign, neatly lettered in

160
00:05:40,892 --> 00:05:42,041
German and Cyrillic.

161
00:05:42,624 --> 00:05:44,555
Entry is forbidden by order of the

162
00:05:44,575 --> 00:05:45,646
Commanding General.

163
00:05:46,216 --> 00:05:49,456
The three cables were tapped on May 11,

164
00:05:49,476 --> 00:05:51,713
May 21, and August 2, 1955.

165
00:05:52,633 --> 00:05:53,714
And then they listened.

166
00:05:54,334 --> 00:05:57,722
The three cables held 273 pairs of wires,

167
00:05:57,742 --> 00:05:59,468
about 1,200 channels.

168
00:05:59,938 --> 00:06:02,589
On average, 121 voice circuits and 28

169
00:06:02,609 --> 00:06:04,827
teletype circuits were recorded

170
00:06:04,847 --> 00:06:05,766
continuously.

171
00:06:06,336 --> 00:06:08,997
About 50,000 reels of magnetic tape were

172
00:06:09,017 --> 00:06:09,332
used.

173
00:06:09,802 --> 00:06:11,139
Some 25 tons.

174
00:06:11,708 --> 00:06:14,547
In London, at the peak, 317 people worked

175
00:06:14,567 --> 00:06:15,872
on the voice tapes.

176
00:06:16,342 --> 00:06:18,645
Three hundred sixty-eight thousand Soviet

177
00:06:18,665 --> 00:06:20,740
conversations were fully transcribed.

178
00:06:21,210 --> 00:06:23,911
In Washington, 350 people worked on the

179
00:06:23,931 --> 00:06:25,096
teletype traffic.

180
00:06:25,667 --> 00:06:28,028
The reports, code-named Regal, covered

181
00:06:28,048 --> 00:06:30,597
Soviet forces in East Germany and Poland,

182
00:06:30,617 --> 00:06:32,979
and identified several thousand Soviet

183
00:06:32,999 --> 00:06:33,543
officers.

184
00:06:34,112 --> 00:06:36,754
Processing went on until September 1958,

185
00:06:36,774 --> 00:06:39,149
more than two years after the tunnel

186
00:06:39,169 --> 00:06:39,614
closed.

187
00:06:40,084 --> 00:06:43,098
The total cost of the project was $6.7

188
00:06:43,118 --> 00:06:43,736
million.

189
00:06:44,656 --> 00:06:45,865
It ended with rain.

190
00:06:46,485 --> 00:06:49,325
In April 1956, after heavy rains, cables

191
00:06:49,345 --> 00:06:52,185
around Berlin flooded and began to fail.

192
00:06:52,655 --> 00:06:54,673
Soviet signal troops and East German

193
00:06:54,693 --> 00:06:56,824
technicians worked frantically to keep

194
00:06:56,844 --> 00:06:58,125
communications running.

195
00:06:58,695 --> 00:07:01,433
At about 12:50 on the morning of April 22,

196
00:07:01,453 --> 00:07:04,125
the watchers at the site saw 40 or 50 men

197
00:07:04,145 --> 00:07:06,619
digging along the road, right over the

198
00:07:06,639 --> 00:07:07,013
cable.

199
00:07:07,584 --> 00:07:09,577
At two o'clock, they found the roof of the

200
00:07:09,597 --> 00:07:10,153
tap chamber.

201
00:07:10,623 --> 00:07:12,876
At 2:10, Russian speech came through the

202
00:07:12,896 --> 00:07:13,501
microphone.

203
00:07:13,971 --> 00:07:15,811
At first, they thought it was a manhole

204
00:07:15,831 --> 00:07:17,052
covering a repeater point.

205
00:07:17,622 --> 00:07:19,588
By 4:15, the tone had changed.

206
00:07:20,058 --> 00:07:22,496
One Soviet signals officer told another on

207
00:07:22,516 --> 00:07:24,720
the phone, when we speak we must do so

208
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:25,305
carefully.

209
00:07:25,775 --> 00:07:27,144
We know what the matter is.

210
00:07:27,714 --> 00:07:29,576
Early that morning, someone at the hole

211
00:07:29,596 --> 00:07:30,880
said it for the first time.

212
00:07:31,463 --> 00:07:32,688
The cable is tapped.

213
00:07:33,257 --> 00:07:35,484
They feared a box on the floor was a booby

214
00:07:35,504 --> 00:07:35,751
trap.

215
00:07:36,221 --> 00:07:38,485
One officer suggested tearing it away with

216
00:07:38,505 --> 00:07:39,409
a grappling iron.

217
00:07:39,879 --> 00:07:42,078
If there is no explosion, he said, then we

218
00:07:42,098 --> 00:07:44,032
can calmly go ahead and deal with it.

219
00:07:44,602 --> 00:07:46,847
Twice they noticed the microphone, and did

220
00:07:46,867 --> 00:07:47,763
not recognize it.

221
00:07:48,233 --> 00:07:50,438
The first time, someone said, that is not

222
00:07:50,458 --> 00:07:51,143
a microphone.

223
00:07:51,714 --> 00:07:53,605
Just before noon, a German worker

224
00:07:53,625 --> 00:07:56,038
exclaimed, the box is an entry to a shaft!

225
00:07:56,508 --> 00:07:58,358
The padlock on the trap door was

226
00:07:58,378 --> 00:08:00,229
identified as of English origin.

227
00:08:00,798 --> 00:08:02,498
Once inside, they were impressed.

228
00:08:02,968 --> 00:08:04,916
It must have cost a pretty penny, one

229
00:08:04,936 --> 00:08:05,553
German said.

230
00:08:06,023 --> 00:08:08,018
How neatly and tidily they have done it,

231
00:08:08,038 --> 00:08:08,672
said another.

232
00:08:09,242 --> 00:08:11,602
One German indignantly called it a filthy

233
00:08:11,622 --> 00:08:11,950
trick.

234
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:14,484
Another replied, unless one had seen it

235
00:08:14,504 --> 00:08:16,461
for oneself, nobody would believe it.

236
00:08:17,031 --> 00:08:19,056
That afternoon, the tap wires were cut.

237
00:08:19,526 --> 00:08:21,853
At about 3:50, they started dismantling

238
00:08:21,873 --> 00:08:22,756
the microphone.

239
00:08:23,226 --> 00:08:24,862
Shortly afterward, it went dead.

240
00:08:25,782 --> 00:08:26,900
Then came the twist.

241
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:29,623
The planners expected Moscow to keep

242
00:08:29,643 --> 00:08:29,977
quiet.

243
00:08:30,447 --> 00:08:32,930
Instead, the acting Soviet commandant in

244
00:08:32,950 --> 00:08:35,558
Berlin, Colonel Ivan Kotsyuba, invited the

245
00:08:35,578 --> 00:08:37,373
Berlin press corps on a tour.

246
00:08:37,943 --> 00:08:40,229
It became, the history says, the most

247
00:08:40,249 --> 00:08:42,534
highly publicized peacetime espionage

248
00:08:42,554 --> 00:08:45,088
enterprise in modern times before the U-2

249
00:08:45,108 --> 00:08:45,648
incident.

250
00:08:46,118 --> 00:08:48,189
Worldwide reaction was outstandingly

251
00:08:48,209 --> 00:08:49,988
favorable to American prestige.

252
00:08:50,559 --> 00:08:52,809
The tunnel's staff concluded the discovery

253
00:08:52,829 --> 00:08:53,512
was bad luck.

254
00:08:53,982 --> 00:08:56,595
One old cable in very poor condition, and

255
00:08:56,615 --> 00:08:59,036
a long period of unusually heavy rain.

256
00:08:59,606 --> 00:09:02,461
But in 1961, British intelligence officer

257
00:09:02,481 --> 00:09:05,055
George Blake was arrested as a Soviet

258
00:09:05,075 --> 00:09:05,476
agent.

259
00:09:05,946 --> 00:09:08,547
According to the CIA history, he had been

260
00:09:08,567 --> 00:09:11,167
recruited while a prisoner in North Korea

261
00:09:11,187 --> 00:09:11,678
in 1952.

262
00:09:12,248 --> 00:09:14,275
Blake was privy to the tunnel from the

263
00:09:14,295 --> 00:09:15,622
earliest planning stages.

264
00:09:16,205 --> 00:09:19,141
He said he told his Soviet contact about

265
00:09:19,161 --> 00:09:21,948
it in late 1953, when its location was

266
00:09:21,968 --> 00:09:22,539
decided.

267
00:09:23,109 --> 00:09:25,138
So why did the Soviets let it run for

268
00:09:25,158 --> 00:09:25,913
nearly a year?

269
00:09:26,383 --> 00:09:28,871
It is most probable, the CIA wrote in

270
00:09:28,891 --> 00:09:30,904
1968, that we will never know.

271
00:09:31,474 --> 00:09:33,995
In 2024, the agency went further.

272
00:09:34,465 --> 00:09:36,965
Its public account says the KGB let the

273
00:09:36,985 --> 00:09:39,614
tunnel run, then staged the discovery for

274
00:09:39,634 --> 00:09:40,325
propaganda.

275
00:09:40,895 --> 00:09:43,269
A 2020 review by a member of the CIA's

276
00:09:43,289 --> 00:09:45,726
History Staff agrees with a recent book

277
00:09:45,746 --> 00:09:48,246
that the Soviets decided they had to let

278
00:09:48,266 --> 00:09:50,829
it operate, to avoid a premature exposure

279
00:09:50,849 --> 00:09:51,837
of their source.

280
00:09:52,307 --> 00:09:54,532
And the 1968 history says there was no

281
00:09:54,552 --> 00:09:56,837
evidence that the Soviets ever tried to

282
00:09:56,857 --> 00:09:58,905
feed deception material through the

283
00:09:58,925 --> 00:09:59,319
cables.

284
00:09:59,889 --> 00:10:02,421
Blake was sentenced to 42 years in prison.

285
00:10:02,891 --> 00:10:05,608
In 1966 he escaped from a London prison,

286
00:10:05,628 --> 00:10:07,593
and fled to the Soviet Union.

287
00:10:08,063 --> 00:10:10,093
He died in Moscow in 2020.

288
00:10:11,013 --> 00:10:13,081
The paper trail was thin by design.

289
00:10:13,701 --> 00:10:15,927
Allen Dulles had ordered that as little as

290
00:10:15,947 --> 00:10:17,853
possible about the project be put in

291
00:10:17,873 --> 00:10:18,281
writing.

292
00:10:18,751 --> 00:10:20,743
It is probable, the history's author

293
00:10:20,763 --> 00:10:22,700
noted, that few orders have been so

294
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:23,986
conscientiously obeyed.

295
00:10:24,556 --> 00:10:26,986
The history was written in 1968.

296
00:10:27,456 --> 00:10:30,484
A declassified version came out in 2007.

297
00:10:30,954 --> 00:10:34,047
More of it was declassified on appeal in

298
00:10:34,067 --> 00:10:37,315
2022, and the CIA released another copy in

299
00:10:37,335 --> 00:10:37,704
2023.

300
00:10:38,274 --> 00:10:39,808
So what was the Berlin Tunnel?

301
00:10:40,278 --> 00:10:42,611
A joint American and British wiretap, dug

302
00:10:42,631 --> 00:10:44,734
under the border of East Berlin, that

303
00:10:44,754 --> 00:10:47,030
recorded hundreds of thousands of Soviet

304
00:10:47,050 --> 00:10:48,751
and East German conversations.

305
00:10:49,322 --> 00:10:50,984
Betrayed before it was dug.

306
00:10:51,567 --> 00:10:53,752
Found after a long spell of heavy rain.

307
00:10:54,334 --> 00:10:56,807
And, as far as the CIA could tell, never

308
00:10:56,827 --> 00:10:59,051
used by the Soviets to feed it false

309
00:10:59,071 --> 00:10:59,799
information.

310
00:11:00,369 --> 00:11:02,239
Every secret has a release date.

311
00:11:02,822 --> 00:11:05,553
For the Berlin Tunnel, the first one came

312
00:11:05,573 --> 00:11:08,036
from the Soviets themselves, in April

313
00:11:08,056 --> 00:11:08,372
1956.

314
00:11:09,192 --> 00:11:11,152
If there's a government file you want us

315
00:11:11,172 --> 00:11:12,785
to open, tell us in the comments.

316
00:11:13,355 --> 00:11:15,657
Next time: another file, released in full.
